('650739127aaaf31b', 'pogi', 'Bill', 'approved_by_lce', '', 'E-Legis System\n (Paperless Legislative Workflow and Document Management)\nA Capstone Project\n presented to the Faculty of the \nCollege of Information Technology \nIn Partial Fulfillment of the Requirements\n for the Degree Bachelor of Science in Information Technology\n \nBy:\nEmmanuel Guingab\n Rhestly Manuel\n Jhude Mark Meligrito \nMark Abarro \nJanoah Adriel Utanes \nAPPROVAL SHEET\nThe capstone project entitled, “\n E-Legis System (Paperless Legislative Workflow and Document Management)\n” prepared and submitted by the researchers in partial fulfilment of the requirements of the degree, \nBACHELOR OF SCIENCE IN INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \nis hereby endorsed. \nLOIDA F. HERMOSURA CpE, MSIT\nAdviser\nMAGDA VELASCO \nRespondent\nMARLON L. CASTRO\nInstructor\nAPPROVED \nby the Panel on Oral Examination with a grade of\n PASSED. \nFRANCISCO C. EUGENIO JR., DIT\n          \nSHERYL JOY M. RASPADO, MIT\nMember                                                                  Member\nDARIOS B. ALADO, DIT\nChairman\nACCEPTED AND APPROVED\n in partial fulfillment of the requirements for the degree of Bachelor of Science in Information Technology. \nLOIDA F. HERMOSURA CpE, MSIT\nDean, College of Information Technology\n \nDEDICATION\nThis capstone project “\n E-Legis System (Paperless Legislative Workflow and Document Management)\n” is first and foremost dedicated to Almighty God, who provided us with all the strength and wisdom to complete this project. Without His infinite grace and guidance, this would never be possible.\nThis project is also dedicated to our loving parents and families. With your immense love, infinite patience and many sacrifices made on our behalf, we were able to succeed and become who we are today. Thank you for believing in us and for being there through everything with the help that you\'ve provided.\nThis project is dedicated to our respected thesis adviser, Ms. Loida F. Hermosura\n \n and the members of the faculty of the College of Information and Communications Technology. Your mentorship, constructive feedback and valuable expertise contributed to the development process of our system.\nAlso, we would like to dedicate this project to the officials, secretariat and personnel of the Sangguniang Panglungsod of Santiago City, Isabela. Your insight regarding the legislative workflow and willingness to transform digitally gave the basis of our work.\nFinally, this work is for our friends, classmates and future researchers.\nACKNOWLEDGEMENT\nThe researchers would like to express their sincere gratitude to everyone who contributed to the completion of this study. \nFirst and foremost, the researchers thank Almighty God for guidance, strength, and wisdom throughout the development of this project. \nThe researchers also extend their heartfelt gratitude to their thesis adviser, faculty members, and panelists for their valuable suggestions, guidance, and support. \nSpecial thanks are also given to the personnel of the Sangguniang Panglungsod Office and all respondents who provided information and insights necessary for the completion of this study. Lastly, the researchers would like to thank their families and friends for their encouragement and support. \nABSTRACT\nTraditional legislative offices still rely on paper-based processes in handling ordinances, agendas, resolutions, attendance, approvals, and session management. These manual procedures often result in delayed processing, document duplication, difficulty in monitoring workflow status, and inefficient communication among offices. \nThis study developed \nE-Legis: A Paperless Legislative Workflow and Session Management System with Intelligent Document Processing\n, a web-based system designed to automate legislative workflow operations through digital document management, workflow routing, session scheduling, AI-assisted document processing, and blockchain-based document verification. \nThe system allows the Sangguniang Panglungsod Secretariat to manage agendas, monitor workflows, schedule sessions, publish approved ordinances, and generate reports. Council members and presiding officers can review agendas, vote digitally, monitor attendance, and access legislative documents through a tablet-based session interface. Department offices can submit requests and monitor ordinance status in real time. \nThe system integrates Artificial Intelligence features such as smart document classification, AI summarization, AI comment insights, and intelligent search indexing. Additionally, blockchain-inspired SHA-256 hashing is implemented to verify document integrity and detect unauthorized modifications. \nThe study followed the Agile Software Development Methodology to continuously improve system features through iterative development and testing. Evaluation based on ISO 25010 software quality standards showed that the system is functional, reliable, usable, efficient, maintainable, and secure. The researchers concluded that e-LegisAI can significantly improve legislative workflow efficiency, transparency, document security, and paperless governance operations. \nTABLE OF CONTENTS\n·\n         \nAPPROVAL SHEET\n·\n         \nDEDICATION\n·\n         \nACKNOWLEDGEMENT\n·\n         \nABSTRACT\n·\n         \nTable of contents\n·\n         \nList of Figures\n·\n         \nList of Tables\n·\n         \nCHAPTER I – INTRODUCTION\no\n   \nProject Context\no\n   \nPurpose and Description of the Project\no\n   \nObjectives of the Project\no\n   \nScope and Limitation of the Project\n·\n         \nCHAPTER II - REVIEW OF RELATED LITERATURE\no\n   \nRelated Literature / Theoretical Background\n§\n  \nForeign\n§\n  \nLocal\no\n   \nRelated Studies\n§\n  \nForeign\n§\n  \nLocal\n·\n         \nCHAPTER III – TECHNICAL BACKGROUND\no\n   \nTechnicality of the Project\no\n   \nDetails of Technologies to be used\no\n   \nHow the project will work\n·\n         \nCHAPTER IV – METHODOLOGY\no\n   \nSoftware Development Methodology\no\n   \nEnvironment\n§\n  \nLocale\n§\n  \nPopulation of the Study\n§\n  \nOrganizational Chart/Profile\no\n   \nRequirements Specification\n§\n  \nOperational Feasibility\n·\n         \nFishbone Diagram\n·\n         \nFunctional Decomposition Diagram\n§\n  \nTechnical Feasibility\n·\n         \nCompatibility checking (Hardware/Software and other technologies)\n·\n         \nRelevance of the technologies\n§\n  \nSchedule Feasibility\n·\n         \nGantt Chart\n§\n  \nEconomic Feasibility\n·\n         \nCost and Benefits Analysis\n§\n  \nRequirements Modeling\n·\n         \nData Processing Model\no\n   \nContext Diagram\no\n   \nDataflow Diagram\n·\n         \nObject Modeling\no\n   \nUse Case Diagram\nDesign\nOutput and User-Interface Design(wireframe)\n·\n         \nForms\n·\n         \nReports\nData Design\n·\n         \nEntity Relationship Diagram\n·\n         \nData Dictionary\nSystem Architecture\n·\n         \nNetwork Model\n·\n         \nSecurity\no\n   \nDevelopment\nSoftware Specification\nHardware Specification\nProgram Specification\nProgramming Environment\n·\n         \nFront End\n·\n         \nBack End\n§\n  \nDeployment Diagram\no\n   \nDevelopment Testing\n§\n  \nData Gathering and Procedure\n·\n         \nInternet Method\n·\n         \nQuestionnaire Method\n·\n         \nInterview\n§\n  \nData Analysis\no\n   \nTesting\n§\n  \nUnit Testing\n§\n  \nIntegration Testing\n·\n         \nPerformance Testing\n·\n         \nCHAPTER V – RESULT AND DISCUSSION\n·\n         \nCHAPTER VI – CONCLUSION AND RECOMMENDATION\n·\n         \nBibliography\n·\n         \nAppendices\no\n   \nRelevance Source Code\no\n   \nEvaluation Tool(Quality Assessment Questionnaire)\no\n   \nUsers Guide\no\n   \nCurriculum Vitae\n·\n         \nGlossary\nCHAPTER I \n1.1 INTRODUCTION\nProject Context\nLegislative offices play a vital role in managing ordinances, resolutions, agendas, committee reports, and session proceedings. However, many local government offices still use traditional paper-based methods for handling legislative documents and workflow processes. These manual practices often lead to delayed approvals, misplaced documents, inefficient tracking, and communication difficulties among departments. The Sangguniang Panglungsod office processes a large volume of legislative documents and administrative transactions daily. Managing these documents manually consumes time, increases operational costs, and limits transparency and accessibility. With the advancement of information technology, digital transformation has become essential for improving government services. Paperless management systems provide faster document routing, centralized storage, real-time monitoring, and improved workflow efficiency. \n1.2 Purpose and Description of the Project\nThe study aims to develop a system that addresses inefficiencies in traditional legislative workflow management. To address these issues, the researchers proposed e-LegisAI: A Paperless Legislative Workflow and Session Management System with Intelligent Document Processing. The system integrates workflow automation, session management, AI-assisted document processing, and blockchain-inspired document verification to modernize legislative operations. \nStatement of the Problem\nThe study aims to develop a system that addresses inefficiencies in traditional legislative workflow management.\nSpecifically, the study seeks to answer the following questions: \nHow can legislative documents and workflows be managed digitally and efficiently? \nHow can the system improve agenda preparation, workflow routing, and session management? \nHow can AI features assist in document classification, summarization, and smart searching? \nHow can blockchain verification improve document integrity and transparency? \nHow acceptable is the system based on ISO 25010 software quality standards? \n1.3 Objectives of the Project\nThe general objective is to develop a paperless legislative workflow and session management system with intelligent document processing for legislative offices. The specific objectives are: \nTo develop a user management module for different system users. \nTo develop a document and agenda management module. \nTo implement workflow routing and approval processes. \nTo implement session scheduling, attendance, and digital voting features. \nTo integrate AI-assisted document processing features. \nTo implement blockchain-inspired document verification using SHA-256 hashing. \nTo evaluate the system using ISO 25010 software quality standards. \nScope and Limitation of the Project\nThe study focuses on the development of a web-based legislative workflow and session management system. \nScope\n: \nUser management \nAgenda and ordinance management \nWorkflow routing and approval \nSession scheduling and attendance \nAI summarization and classification \nBlockchain verification \nNotification system \nReports and analytics \nLimitations\n: \nThe system requires internet or local network connectivity. \nAI outputs depend on available document data. \nBlockchain implementation focuses only on document integrity verification. \nThe system is intended only for legislative office operations. \nCHAPTER II \n 2.1 REVIEW OF RELATED LITERATURE\n2.1.2 Related Literature / Theoretical Background\nPaperless Management Systems:\n Paperless systems reduce physical document dependency and improve organizational efficiency through digital workflows. \nLegislative Workflow Systems:\n Legislative workflow systems automate document routing, approvals, and session monitoring. \nArtificial Intelligence in Document Processing:\n AI technologies improve classification, summarization, and search functions. \nBlockchain in Government Systems:\n Blockchain enhances transparency, integrity verification, and tamper detection. \n2.1.3 Related Studies\nForeign Studies:\n Several foreign studies highlighted the importance of workflow automation and AI-assisted systems in improving governance efficiency. \nLocal Studies:\n Local studies emphasized the need for digital transformation in government offices to improve document monitoring and service delivery. \nCHAPTER III \n3.1 TECHNICAL BACKGROUND\nTechnicality of the Project\n(Placeholder for additional technical context)\n3.1.1Details of Technologies to be used\nThe system follows the MVC (Model-View-Controller) architecture. The required technologies are readily available and compatible with existing hardware infrastructure. \nPHP MVC \nMySQL \nCSS and Bootstrap 5 \nJavaScript and AJAX \nChart.js \nBlockchain Hash Verification (SHA-256 Hashing) \nSpeech-to-Text AI \n3.1.2 How the project will work\nInput:\n Legislative documents, User information, Session schedules, Ordinance requests. \nProcess:\n AI processing, Workflow routing, Blockchain verification, and Session management. \nOutput:\n Approved ordinances, Reports, notifications, and verified legislative records. \nCHAPTER IV \n4.1 METHODOLOGY\n4.1.1 Software Development Methodology\nThe researchers adopted the Agile Software Development Methodology in developing the E-Legis System. Agile was selected because it allows continuous development, testing, and improvement of the system through iterative cycles. The methodology enables frequent \ncommunication with stakeholders from the Sangguniang Panglungsod to ensure that the system requirements are met and that improvements can be incorporated throughout the development process. \nThe Agile methodology consists of the following phases: \n1\n. Requirements Definition & Analysis of Concepts\n: Gathering initial problem statements, stakeholder expectations, and system requirements from key users (e.g., SP Secretariat, Council Members, Presiding Officer).\n2. \nPlanning of Sprints\n: Organizing user stories into prioritized backlogs and scheduling specific features to be designed, coded, and tested within short 2-week sprint cycles.\n3\n. Collaborative Design & Development\n: Engineering system modules (such as PHP MVC components, document hashing, and database schemas) through continuous collaboration with end-users.\n4\n. Create & Implemen\nt: Executing feature builds, running unit and integration tests, gathering user feedback, and deploying functional software increments frequently.\n5. \nReview & Monitor\n: Monitoring system performance, evaluating user feedback against business goals, and feeding improvements back into Phase 1 for the next sprint cycle.\nThis circular process ensures that system features evolve dynamically based on real-time testing, risk reduction, and continuous stakeholder feedback.\n4.2 Environment\nThe E-Legis System was developed in a web-based environment accessible through modern web browsers. The system operates within the Local Government Unit network and can be deployed through web hosting services for wider accessibility. \nComponent\nSpecification\nOperating System\nWindows 11 \nWeb Server\nApache \nProgramming Language\nPHP \nDatabase\nMySQL \nFront-End\nHTML, CSS, JavaScript \nDevelopment Pattern\nMVC Architecture \nBrowser\nGoogle Chrome, Microsoft Edge \n4.2.1 Locale of the Study\nThe study was conducted at the Sangguniang Panglungsod of Santiago City, Isabela, where legislative activities such as ordinance creation, agenda preparation, and document management are regularly performed. \n4.2.2 Population of the Study\nThe respondents of the study consist of the Sangguniang Panglungsod Secretariat, Council Members, Department Personnel, and Administrative Staff. \nUser Type\nPurpose\nSP Secretariat\nSystem Administration \nPresiding Officer\nAgenda Approval \nMayor\'s Office\nOrdinance Submission \nCouncil Members\nAgenda Viewing, create legislative bills \n4.2.3 \nOrganizational Chart/Profile\n                                                                                            organizational chart \nSecretary to the City Council Organizational Chart\nThis organizational chart outlines the administrative structure of the \nSecretary to the City Council Secretariat\n, which operates under the leadership of the \nOIC - Secretary to the \nSanggunian\n (Mary Magdalena H. Velasco) and is divided into four functional sections:\nCommittee Affairs Section:\n Handles legislative committee coordination, legal assistance, and administrative support.\nRecords Section:\n Manages legislative document archiving, record-keeping, and official files.\nJournals Section:\n Oversees session transcription, stenographic duties, and journal tracking.\nSupport Section:\n Provides general office administration, clerical aid, and operational assistance.\nStaffing Structure:\n The workforce consists of regular \nPermanent\n staff (solid borders, ranging from SG3 to SG18) and \nCasual\n personnel (red dashed borders, primarily filling Administrative Aide roles across all four sections). Key executive and technical roles currently remain \nVacant\n.\n4.3 Requirements Specification\nFunctional Requirements\n: \nThe system shall: \nAllow user authentication and authorization. \nAllow the Mayor\'s Office to submit ordinances. \nAllow the SP Secretariat to manage users. \nAllow the SP Secretariat to create agendas. \nAllow the Presiding Officer to approve agendas. \nAllow Council Members to view approved agendas. \nGenerate reports and archives. \nTrack document versions. \nDetect document tampering through Blockchain. \nConvert recorded sessions into text using Speech-to-Text AI. \nNon-Functional Requirements\n: \nSecurity \nReliability \nPerformance Efficiency \nUsability \nMaintainability \n4.3.1 Operational Feasibility\nThe proposed system is operationally feasible because it automates manual legislative processes and reduces dependency on paper documents. The users already perform similar tasks manually; therefore, transitioning to a digital platform is practical and beneficial. \n-762003175000Fishbone diagram\nFishbone diagram\nFishbone Diagram \nThe Fishbone Diagram identifies the root causes of inefficiency in traditional legislative workflow systems. The additional AI Features category highlights the absence of intelligent technologies such as AI tampering alerts and voice-to-text transcription. These features are important because they improve document security, automate session transcription, and reduce manual preparation of session minutes. \nFunctional Decomposition Diagram \nThe E-Legis System is a comprehensive legislative management platform that decomposes its operations into eight core modules: User Management, Legislative Document Management, Session Management, Tablet Mode, Voting & Review, Notification, Publication & Reporting, and Security & Verification.\n                                                                                                                           ERD\nThis diagram shows how the Kaya Portal database is organized, mapping out how users, meetings, attendance, agendas, and legislative records connect to each other.\n4.3.2 Technical Feasibility\nThe technologies required for development are readily available and compatible with existing hardware infrastructure.\nTechnologies Used\n \nPHP MVC  \nMySQL  \nCSS\nJavaScript  \nAJAX  \nBlockchain Hash Verification  \nSpeech-to-Text AI  \nEconomic Feasibility\n \nThe implementation of E-Legis reduces: \nPrinting expenses  \nArchiving costs  \nPhysical storage requirements  \nDocument retrieval time  \nBenefits include: \nFaster processing  \nImproved transparency  \nEnhanced security  \n4.3.3 \nSchedule Feasibility\nGant chart \nGant chart \nEconomic Feasibility\nRequirements Modeling\nContext diagram level 0\nThis Context Diagram illustrates the \ne legis\n system data flow, showing how administrators or presiding officers log in, manage user information, create sessions, and handle agenda workflows while council members log in, create and upload bills, and attend sessions.\nData flow diagram level 1\nThis Level 1 Data Flow Diagram illustrates how the Admin, Council Members, and Proceeding Officers interact with six core system processes—spanning user management, legislative templates, agenda workflows, voting, notifications, and approvals—to read and store structured data across six dedicated databases.\n4.7 \nDesign\nOutput and User-Interface Design(wireframe)\nForms\nReports\nData Design\nEntity Relationship Diagram\nData Dictionary\nSystem Architecture\nNetwork Model\nSecurity\n4.8 \nDevelopment\nSoftware Specification\nHardware Specification\nProgram Specification\nProgramming Environment\nFront End\nBack End\nDeployment Diagram\n4.9 Development Testing\nData Gathering and Procedure\n: \nInterview Method:\n \nInterviews were conducted with personnel from the Sangguniang Panglungsod to gather information regarding the current legislative workflow. \nQuestionnaire Method:\n Questionnaires based on ISO 25010 standards were distributed to respondents during system evaluation. \nInternet Method:\n Relevant journals, government publications, and online resources were reviewed to support the development of the system. \nObservations and Consultations:\n Conducted with legislative office personnel. \nTesting\n: \nUnit Testing:\n Each module was tested individually. \nIntegration Testing:\n \nModules were tested collectively to ensure proper interaction. \nPerformance Testing:\n \nThe system\'s response time and functionality were evaluated. \nLoad Testing:\n \nThe system was tested under multiple user requests.', '', '7486bc1d2df7e69c', 'isu', 'council members', '2026-09-22 22:00:19.000000', '2026-10-05 23:24:09.000000', '8f83bf3d65b7b0b733481027ca619ce100f89b9e2b8b76312ac1c46e95e2f477', 'b4f39005182a0122', 'mayor', '2026-10-05 23:24:09.000000', 0, NULL, 'manuscript.update.docx', 4452198, 'application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.wordprocessingml.document', 'd158a2f2916603ee_manuscript.update.docx', NULL, '7486bc1d2df7e69c', 'isu', '2026-09-27 00:18:10.000000', 'abdeab56a2f2c2a1192a7f215f032e19247149377dcc7b5fe5d9e6d74ba0c5ac', NULL, NULL, 'Legislative Matters', 'Archived for Local Download', 'Approved Resolutions/Ordinances', NULL, NULL, 'Pending', NULL, 'LCE Approved', '2026-10-05 23:24:09.000000', 'b4f39005182a0122', 'FILE - Local Offline Archive', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'Filing/Scan', NULL, NULL, 'LEG-2026-650739127AAA', '2026-09-22 22:00:19.000000', 'isu', 'council members', NULL, 'Secretariat', 'secretariat', 1, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'LEG-2026-65073912', '2026-09-22 22:00:19.000000', NULL, NULL, NULL),
