('8e8aa1562788ec23', '', 2, 'Republic of the Philippines City of Santiago -000-', 'Republic of the Philippines City of Santiago -000-<br><br>OFFICE OF THE SECRETARY - SANGGUNIANG PANLUNGSOD<br><br>EXCERPT FROM THE MINUTES OF THE 4TH REGULAR SESSION<br><br>OF THE 12TH CITY COUNCIL HELD ON AUGUST 1 1, 2025 AT SP SESSION HALL,<br><br>2ND FLOOR, CITY HALL, SAN ANDRES, SANTIAGO CITY<br><br>APPROVED UNDER SUSPENDED RULES<br><br>Sponsored by: Coun. Cassandra Eunice Sable<br><br>Co-sponsored by: Coun. Tomirose Bautista-dela Cruz Coun. Sherman A. Miguel<br><br>Coun. Anton Phoenix A. Abaya<br><br>Coun. Joan Mae V. de Jesus<br><br>Coun. Jay Arcie S. Co<br><br>Coun.Russel D. Ponce<br><br>Coun. Carlo N. de Luna<br><br>Coun.Orlando T. Chan<br><br>Coun. Benzi Tan Chai<br><br>Coun. Christopher G. Respicio<br><br>Coun. Bella M. Yodyod<br><br>RESOLUTION NO. 12THCC-023<br><br>AUTHORIZING THE HONORABLE CITY MAYOR, ATTY. ALYSSA SHEENA P. TAN, CPA, FOR AND ON BEHALF OF THE CITY GOVERNMENT OF SANTIAGO, TO ENTER INTO AND SIGN THE MEMORANDUM OF AGREEMENT (MOA) WITH THE COMMISSION ON POPULATION AND DEVELOPMENT REGION 11,<br><br>RELATIVE TO THE UTILIZATION OF THE 2025 RAFAEL M. SALAS KAUNLARANG PANTAO AWARD (RMSKPA) CASH GRANT<br><br>WHEREAS, the 2025 Rafael M. Salas Kaunlarang Pantao Award (RMSKPA) is a recognition and award program for institutions and individuals who have significantly contributed to the advancement of the Population and Development Agenda. With the devolution policy, the role of Local Government Units (LGUs) has been more pronounced in advancing the Philippine Population and Development Plan of Action specially in institutionalizing and localizing Population Development (POPDEV) strategies;<br><br>WHEREAS, the 2025 Rafael M. Salas Kaunlarang Pantao Award (RMSKPA) is a prestigious recognition that honors the Most Outstanding LGUs in the country for their exemplary efforts in mainstreaming and localizing the Population and Development Agenda within their local governance systems;<br><br>WHEREAS, the Philippine Population and Development Plan of Action (PPD-POA) 2023-2028 serves as the overall blueprint for inter-agency population challenges in order to accelerate the attainment of the Administration\'s socio-economic development agenda under PDP 2023-2028;<br><br>WHEREAS, by virtue of a Presidential and Cabinet Directive issued in 2019, the Commission on Population and Development (CPD) is directed to coordinate with relevant government agencies and LGUs for the intensified implementation of the Philippine Population and Development Program (PPDP) at all levels;<br><br>WHEREAS, the Local Government Unit of Santiago City has been adjudged as the Regional Winner of the 2025 Rafael M. Salas Kaunlarang Pantao Award (RMSKPA) — Search for the Most Outstanding LGUs in Localizing the Population and Development (POPDEV) Agenda (city category), thereby qualifying to receive a cash grant amounting to Fifty Thousand Pesos (P50,OOO.OO)•,<br><br>Page I of2 pages ofResolution No. 12thCC-02 l!åj<br><br>WHEREAS, a Memorandum of Agreement (MOA) between the City Government of Santiago and the Commission on Population and Development Region Il aims to improve the policy and program implementation at the local level and ensure localization and institutionalization of population-related strategies and interventions;<br><br>WHEREAS, under Section 8 paragraph (b) (1) (f) of Republic Act No. 7720, the City Mayor shall represent the City in all its business transactions and sign on its behalf all bonds, contracts, and obligations, and such other documents upon authority of the Sangguniang Panlungsod or under existing laws or ordinances;<br><br>WHEREFORE, on motion of Coun. Cassandra Eunice Sable, unanimously seconded, be it<br><br>RESOLVED, as it is hereby resolved, to authorize Honorable City Mayor, Atty. Alyssa Sheena P. Tan, CPA, for and on behalf of the City Government of Santiago, to enter into and sign the Memorandum of Agreement (MOA) with the Commission on Population and Development Region Il, relative to the utilization of the 2025 Rafael M. Salas Kaunlarang Pantao Award (RMSKPA) Cash Grant.<br><br>RESOLVED FURTHER, to furnish copies of this Resolution to all persons, offices, and government agencies concerned for their information and guidance.<br><br>ADOPTED. AUGUST 11, 2025.<br><br>CERTIFIED TRUE AND CORRECT:<br><br>MARY M A H. VELASCO or-s r t o the Sanggunian<br><br>ATTESTED:<br><br>A <br><br>JAMAYNE LO TAN<br><br> City Vice Mayor residing Officer<br><br>APPROVED:<br><br>ATTY. ALYSSA SHE NA P. TAN, CPA<br><br>City M r<br><br>Page 2 of2 pages ofResolution No. 12thCC-023', 'R12thCC-023 MOA with the Commission on Population and Development Region II Relative to the Utilization of the 2025 Rafael M. Salas Kaunlarang Pantao Award RMSKPA Cash Grant.docx', 'revision_15024d9210eb8722_v2_9e6e1aa1ed259961_R12thCC-023_MOA_with_the_Commission_on_Population_and_Development_Region_II_Relative_to_the_Utilization_of_the_2025_Rafael_M._Salas_Kaunlarang_Pantao_Award_RMSKPA_Cash_Grant.docx', '21a77db1f7d4528037e04616df9da6f1d5b34c2f638cb86a6f7433bc2c56cc59', '7486bc1d2df7e69c', 'isu', '2026-10-06 01:30:11.000000', NULL, NULL, '15024d9210eb8722', 'Revision v2'),
('90ddaf6908268ae6', '23ec31dcbe6fd91d', 1, 'adsawd', 'E-LEGISAI:\r\n A PAPERLESS LEGISLATIVE WORKFLOW AND SESSION MANAGEMENT \r\nSYSTEM WITH INTELLIGENT DOCUMENT PROCESSING \r\nA Capstone Project\r\n presented to the \r\nFaculty\r\n of the \r\nCollege of Information Technology \r\nIn Partial \r\nFulfillment\r\n of the Requirements\r\n for the Degree Bachelor of Science in Information Technology\r\n \r\nBy:\r\nEmmanuel Guingab\r\n \r\nRhestly\r\n Manuel\r\n \r\nJhude\r\n Mark Meligrito \r\nMark Abarro \r\nJanoah Adriel Utanes\r\n \r\nAPPROVAL SHEET\r\nThe capstone project entitled, “\r\nE-\r\nLegisAI\r\n: A Paperless Legislative Workflow and Session Management System with Intelligent Document Processing\r\n” prepared and submitted by the researchers in partial fulfilment of the requirements of the degree, \r\nBACHELOR OF SCIENCE IN INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \r\nis hereby endorsed. \r\nLOIDA F. HERMOSURA \r\nCpE\r\n, MSIT\r\nAdviser\r\nFERDINAND MARCOS JR.\r\nRespondent\r\nMARLON L. CASTRO\r\nInstructor\r\nAPPROVED \r\nby the Panel on Oral Examination with a grade of\r\n PASSED.\r\n \r\nFRANCISCO C. EUGENIO JR., DIT\r\n          \r\nSHERYL JOY M. RASPADO, MIT\r\nMember                                                                  \r\nMember\r\nDARIOS B. ALADO, DIT\r\nChairman\r\nACCEPTED AND APPROVED\r\n in partial \r\nfulfillment\r\n of the requirements for the degree of Bachelor of Science in Information Technology. \r\nLOIDA F. HERMOSURA \r\nCpE\r\n, MSIT\r\nDean, College of Information Technology\r\n \r\nDEDICATION\r\nThis capstone project “\r\nE-\r\nLegisAI\r\n: A Paperless Legislative Workflow and Session Management System with Intelligent Document Processing\r\n” is first and foremost dedicated to Almighty God, who provided us with all the strength and wisdom to complete this project. Without His infinite grace and guidance, this would never be possible.\r\nThis project is also dedicated to our loving parents and families. With your immense love, infinite patience and many sacrifices made on our behalf, we were able to succeed and become who we are today. Thank you for believing in us and for being there through everything with the help that you\'ve provided.\r\nThis project is dedicated to our respected thesis adviser, Mr. Jay-\r\nar\r\n \r\nMesquiola\r\n and the members of the faculty of the College of Information and Communications Technology. Your mentorship, constructive feedback and valuable expertise contributed to the development process of our system.\r\nAlso, we would like to dedicate this project to the officials, secretariat and personnel of the Sangguniang \r\nPanglungsod\r\n of Santiago City, Isabela. Your insight regarding the legislative workflow and willingness to transform digitally gave the basis of our work.\r\nFinally, this work is for our friends, classmates and future researchers.\r\nACKNOWLEDGEMENT\r\nThe researchers would like to express their sincere gratitude to everyone who contributed to the completion of this study. \r\nFirst and foremost, the researchers thank Almighty God for guidance, strength, and wisdom throughout the development of this project. \r\nThe researchers also extend their heartfelt gratitude to their thesis adviser, faculty members, and \r\npanelists\r\n for their valuable suggestions, guidance, and support. \r\nSpecial thanks are also given to the personnel of the Sangguniang \r\nPanglungsod\r\n Office and all respondents who provided information and insights necessary for the completion of this study. Lastly, the researchers would like to thank their families and friends for their encouragement and support. \r\nABSTRACT\r\nTraditional legislative offices still rely on paper-based processes in handling ordinances, agendas, resolutions, attendance, approvals, and session management. These manual procedures often result in delayed processing, document duplication, difficulty in monitoring workflow status, and inefficient communication among offices. \r\nThis study developed \r\nE-\r\nLegisAI\r\n: A Paperless Legislative Workflow and Session Management System with Intelligent Document Processing\r\n, a web-based system designed to automate legislative workflow operations through digital document management, workflow routing, session scheduling, AI-assisted document processing, and blockchain-based document verification. \r\nThe system allows the Sangguniang \r\nPanglungsod\r\n Secretariat to manage agendas, monitor workflows, schedule sessions, publish approved ordinances, and generate reports. Council members and presiding officers can review agendas, vote digitally, monitor attendance, and access legislative documents through a tablet-based session interface. Department offices can submit requests and monitor ordinance status in real time. \r\nThe system integrates Artificial Intelligence features such as smart document classification, AI summarization, AI comment insights, and intelligent search indexing. Additionally, blockchain-inspired SHA-256 hashing is implemented to verify document integrity and detect unauthorized modifications. \r\nThe study followed the Agile Software Development Methodology to continuously improve system features through iterative development and testing. Evaluation based on ISO 25010 software quality standards showed that the system is functional, reliable, usable, efficient, maintainable, and secure. The researchers concluded that e-\r\nLegisAI\r\n can significantly improve legislative workflow efficiency, transparency, document security, and paperless governance operations. \r\nTABLE OF CONTENTS\r\nAPPROVAL SHEET\r\nDEDICATION\r\nACKNOWLEDGEMENT\r\nABSTRACT\r\nTable of contents\r\nCHAPTER I – INTRODUCTION\r\nProject Context\r\nPurpose and Description of the Project\r\nObjectives of the Project\r\nScope and Limitation of the Project\r\nCHAPTER II - REVIEW OF RELATED LITERATURE\r\nRelated Literature / Theoretical Background\r\nForeign\r\nLocal\r\nRelated Studies\r\nForeign\r\nLocal\r\nCHAPTER III – TECHNICAL BACKGROUND\r\nTechnicality of the Project\r\nDetails of Technologies to be used\r\nHow the project will work\r\nCHAPTER IV – METHODOLOGY\r\nSoftware Development Methodology\r\nEnvironment\r\nLocale\r\nPopulation of the Study\r\nOrganizational Chart/Profile\r\nRequirements Specification\r\nFunctional Requirements\r\nNon-Functional Requirements\r\nOperational Feasibility\r\nFishbone Diagram\r\nFunctional Decomposition Diagram\r\nTechnical Feasibility\r\nTechnologies Used\r\nEconomic Feasibility\r\n \r\nCost and Benefits Analysis\r\n            Development Testing\r\nData Gathering and Procedure\r\nInterview Method\r\nQuestionnaire Method\r\nInternet Method\r\nObservation and \r\nConclutions\r\n   Testing\r\nUnit Testing\r\nIntegration Testing\r\nPerformance Testing\r\nLoad Testing\r\nCHAPTER I \r\n1.1 INTRODUCTION\r\nProject Context\r\nLegislative offices play a vital role in managing ordinances, resolutions, agendas, committee reports, and session proceedings. However, many local government offices still use traditional paper-based methods for handling legislative documents and workflow processes. These manual practices often lead to delayed approvals, misplaced documents, inefficient tracking, and communication difficulties among departments. The Sangguniang \r\nPanglungsod\r\n office processes a large volume of legislative documents and administrative transactions daily. Managing these documents manually consumes time, increases operational costs, and limits transparency and accessibility. With the advancement of information technology, digital transformation has become essential for improving government services. Paperless management systems provide faster document routing, centralized storage, real-time monitoring, and improved workflow efficiency. \r\n1.2 Purpose and Description of the Project\r\nThe study aims to develop a system that addresses inefficiencies in traditional legislative workflow management. To address these issues, the researchers proposed e-\r\nLegisAI\r\n: A Paperless Legislative Workflow and Session Management System with Intelligent Document Processing. The system integrates workflow automation, session management, AI-assisted document processing, and blockchain-inspired document verification to modernize legislative operations. \r\nStatement of the Problem\r\nThe study aims to develop a system that addresses inefficiencies in traditional legislative workflow management.\r\nSpecifically, the study seeks to answer the following questions: \r\nHow can legislative documents and workflows be managed digitally and efficiently? \r\nHow can the system improve agenda preparation, workflow routing, and session management? \r\nHow can AI features assist in document classification, summarization, and smart searching? \r\nHow can blockchain verification improve document integrity and transparency? \r\nHow acceptable is the system based on ISO 25010 software quality standards? \r\n1.3 Objectives of the Project\r\nThe general objective is to develop a paperless legislative workflow and session management system with intelligent document processing for legislative offices. The specific objectives are: \r\nTo develop a user management module for different system users. \r\nTo develop a document and agenda management module. \r\nTo implement workflow routing and approval processes. \r\nTo implement session scheduling, attendance, and digital voting features. \r\nTo integrate AI-assisted document processing features. \r\nTo implement blockchain-inspired document verification using SHA-256 hashing. \r\nTo evaluate the system using ISO 25010 software quality standards. \r\nScope and Limitation of the Project\r\nThe study focuses on the development of a web-based legislative workflow and session management system. \r\nScope\r\n: \r\nUser management \r\nAgenda and ordinance management \r\nWorkflow routing and approval \r\nSession scheduling and attendance \r\nAI summarization and classification \r\nBlockchain verification \r\nNotification system \r\nReports and analytics \r\nLimitations\r\n: \r\nThe system requires internet or local network connectivity. \r\nAI outputs depend on available document data. \r\nBlockchain implementation focuses only on document integrity verification. \r\nThe system is intended only for legislative office operations. \r\nCHAPTER II \r\n 2.1 REVIEW OF RELATED LITERATURE\r\n2.1.2 Related Literature / Theoretical Background\r\nPaperless Management Systems:\r\n Paperless systems reduce physical document dependency and improve organizational efficiency through digital workflows. \r\nLegislative Workflow Systems:\r\n Legislative workflow systems automate document routing, approvals, and session monitoring. \r\nArtificial Intelligence in Document Processing:\r\n AI technologies improve classification, summarization, and search functions. \r\nBlockchain in Government Systems:\r\n Blockchain enhances transparency, integrity verification, and tamper detection. \r\n2.1.3 Related Studies\r\nForeign Studies:\r\n Several foreign studies highlighted the importance of workflow automation and AI-assisted systems in improving governance efficiency. \r\nLocal Studies:\r\n Local studies emphasized the need for digital transformation in government offices to improve document monitoring and service delivery. \r\nCHAPTER III \r\n3.1 TECHNICAL BACKGROUND\r\nTechnicality of the Project\r\n(Placeholder for additional technical context)\r\n3.1.1Details of Technologies to be used\r\nThe system follows the MVC (Model-View-Controller) architecture. The required technologies are readily available and compatible with existing hardware infrastructure. \r\nPHP MVC \r\nMySQL \r\nCSS and Bootstrap 5 \r\nJavaScript and AJAX \r\nChart.js \r\nBlockchain Hash Verification (SHA-256 Hashing) \r\nSpeech-to-Text AI \r\n3.1.2 How the project will work\r\nInput:\r\n Legislative documents, User information, Session schedules, Ordinance requests. \r\nProcess:\r\n AI processing, Workflow routing, Blockchain verification, and Session management. \r\nOutput:\r\n Approved ordinances, Reports, notifications, and verified legislative records. \r\nCHAPTER IV \r\n4.1 METHODOLOGY\r\n4.1.1 Software Development Methodology\r\nThe researchers adopted the Agile Software Development Methodology in developing the E-Legis System. Agile was selected because it allows continuous development, testing, and improvement of the system through iterative cycles. The methodology enables frequent communication with stakeholders from the Sangguniang \r\nPanglungsod\r\n to ensure that the system requirements are met and that improvements can be incorporated throughout the development process. \r\nThe Agile methodology consists of the following phases: \r\nPlanning Phase:\r\n Identification of legislative workflow problems; Gathering requirements from the SP Secretariat and other stakeholders; Definition of project scope and objectives. \r\nDesign Phase:\r\n Creation of system architecture; Development of wireframes and database structures; Preparation of process flow diagrams. \r\nDevelopment Phase:\r\n Coding of system modules using PHP MVC; Integration of Blockchain and AI functionalities; Development of role-based access controls. \r\nTesting Phase:\r\n Conducting unit testing, integration testing, and system testing; Evaluation using ISO 25010 standards. \r\nDeployment Phase:\r\n Installation of the system on the server; User orientation and training. \r\nMaintenance Phase:\r\n Bug fixing; Feature enhancement; Security updates. \r\n4.2 Environment\r\nThe E-Legis System was developed in a web-based environment accessible through modern web browsers. The system operates within the Local Government Unit network and can be deployed through web hosting services for wider accessibility. \r\nComponent\r\nSpecification\r\nOperating System\r\nWindows 11 \r\nWeb Server\r\nApache \r\nProgramming Language\r\nPHP \r\nDatabase\r\nMySQL \r\nFront-End\r\nHTML, CSS, JavaScript \r\nDevelopment Pattern\r\nMVC Architecture \r\nBrowser\r\nGoogle Chrome, Microsoft Edge \r\n4.2.1 Locale of the Study\r\nThe study was conducted at the Sangguniang \r\nPanglungsod\r\n of Santiago City, Isabela, where legislative activities such as ordinance creation, agenda preparation, and document management are regularly performed. \r\n4.2.2 Population of the Study\r\nThe respondents of the study consist of the Sangguniang \r\nPanglungsod\r\n Secretariat, Council Members, Department Personnel, and Administrative Staff. \r\nUser Type\r\nPurpose\r\nSP Secretariat\r\nSystem Administration \r\nPresiding Officer\r\nAgenda Approval \r\nMayor\'s Office\r\nOrdinance Submission \r\nCouncil Members\r\nAgenda Viewing, create legislative bills \r\n                                                                                            organizational chart \r\n4.3 Requirements Specification\r\nFunctional Requirements\r\n: \r\nThe system shall: \r\nAllow user authentication and authorization. \r\nAllow the Mayor\'s Office to submit ordinances. \r\nAllow the SP Secretariat to manage users. \r\nAllow the SP Secretariat to create agendas. \r\nAllow the Presiding Officer to approve agendas. \r\nAllow Council Members to view approved agendas. \r\nGenerate reports and archives. \r\nTrack document versions. \r\nDetect document tampering through Blockchain. \r\nConvert recorded sessions into text using Speech-to-Text AI. \r\nNon-Functional Requirements\r\n: \r\nSecurity \r\nReliability \r\nPerformance Efficiency \r\nUsability \r\nMaintainability \r\n4.4 Operational Feasibility\r\nThe proposed system is operationally feasible because it automates manual legislative processes and reduces dependency on paper documents. The users already perform similar tasks manually; therefore, transitioning to a digital platform is practical and beneficial. \r\nFishbone Diagram \r\nThe Fishbone Diagram identifies the root causes of inefficiency in traditional legislative workflow systems. The additional AI Features category highlights the absence of intelligent technologies such as AI tampering alerts and voice-to-text transcription. These features are important because they improve document security, automate session transcription, and reduce manual preparation of session minutes. \r\nFunctional Decomposition Diagram \r\n4.5 Technical Feasibility\r\nThe technologies required for development are readily available and compatible with existing hardware infrastructure.\r\nTechnologies Used\r\n \r\nPHP MVC  \r\nMySQL  \r\nCSS\r\nJavaScript  \r\nAJAX  \r\nBlockchain Hash Verification  \r\nSpeech-to-Text AI  \r\nEconomic Feasibility\r\n \r\nThe implementation of E-Legis reduces: \r\nPrinting expenses  \r\nArchiving costs  \r\nPhysical storage requirements  \r\nDocument retrieval time  \r\nBenefits include: \r\nFaster processing  \r\nImproved transparency  \r\nEnhanced security  \r\n4.6 Development Testing\r\nData Gathering and Procedure\r\n: \r\nInterview Method:\r\n \r\nInterviews were conducted with personnel from the Sangguniang \r\nPanglungsod\r\n to gather information regarding the current legislative workflow. \r\nQuestionnaire Method:\r\n Questionnaires based on ISO 25010 standards were distributed to respondents during system evaluation. \r\nInternet Method:\r\n Relevant journals, government publications, and online resources were reviewed to support the development of the system. \r\nObservations and Consultations:\r\n Conducted with legislative office personnel. \r\nTesting\r\n: \r\nUnit Testing:\r\n Each module was tested individually. \r\nIntegration Testing:\r\n \r\nModules were tested collectively to ensure proper interaction. \r\nPerformance Testing:\r\n \r\nThe system\'s response time and functionality were evaluated. \r\nLoad Testing:\r\n \r\nThe system was tested under multiple user requests.', 'E-Legis manuscipt (2).docx', 'c999eaaf020f2b56_E-Legis_manuscipt__2_.docx', '92b0bb4dad380144cb98f523a9011d1918df2f12972f85a53d6eeb3124db18ed', 'c59939d58ad69391', 'pogi', '2026-07-12 15:55:36.000000', 'Baseline snapshot migrated from existing record', NULL, '23ec31dcbe6fd91d', 'Revision v1'),
('bd640ec27747c541', 'a0f998cc62beda08', 1, 'SP Sec Flowchart (1)', '<p></p>', 'SP Sec Flowchart (1).docx', '40065904eda3fb0b_SP_Sec_Flowchart__1_.docx', 'd6cbe96288b8d827f3786354138f4fc89b7a84657a003f9f463244adcd103d50', 'c59939d58ad69391', 'pogi', '2026-09-23 02:08:40.000000', 'Baseline snapshot migrated from existing record', NULL, 'a0f998cc62beda08', 'Revision v1'),
('c9bf604d6752881d', '0b6d73d15c83effe', 1, 'pogi', '', 'LIVE-RADIO-DRAMA-SKIT-COMPETITION-Year-4-Final-Mechanics (2).pdf', 'ccacdec973bebca9_LIVE-RADIO-DRAMA-SKIT-COMPETITION-Year-4-Final-Mechanics__2_.pdf', '0e77df7113bc857b97c0727dad9c98ab90f1e07ce79c7bccdbc310ee5d6989e5', '7486bc1d2df7e69c', 'isu', '2026-09-13 23:24:28.000000', 'Baseline snapshot migrated from existing record', NULL, '0b6d73d15c83effe', 'Revision v1');
INSERT INTO `legislative_versions` (`id`, `legislative_id`, `version_number`, `title`, `content`, `file_name`, `file_path`, `document_hash`, `created_by_id`, `created_by_name`, `created_at`, `change_reason`, `signed_file_path`, `document_id`, `status`) VALUES
('d25fc048950418f2', '99d2ff63c69bc204', 1, 'pogi', '2.1 Related Literature / Theoretical Background\r\nA. Foreign Related Literature\r\n1. OECD Digital Government Policy Framework\r\nThe \r\nOrganization\r\n for Economic Co-operation and Development (OECD, 2020) presented the Digital Government Policy Framework, which identifies six dimensions of mature digital government: digital by design, data-driven public sector, government as a platform, open by default, user-driven government, and proactive government. The framework emphasizes that digital transformation should not simply convert paper documents into electronic files but should redesign government processes to become more integrated and efficient.\r\nThis literature is relevant to E-Legis because the proposed system does more than store legislative documents digitally. It integrates document routing, agenda preparation, session management, notifications, reporting, and document verification into a single web-based environment.\r\n2. Digital Transformation in Government\r\nThe OECD (2020) explained that digital transformation affects government operations by encouraging the strategic use of digital technologies and data. Government organizations are expected to redesign internal processes, improve coordination, and provide more responsive services rather than merely placing existing services online.\r\nThis supports the development of E-Legis because the system seeks to transform the traditional paper-based legislative workflow into an integrated digital workflow. The system can therefore be viewed as part of the broader movement toward digital government.\r\n3. Electronic Document Management\r\nElectronic document management systems provide organizations with mechanisms for storing, organizing, retrieving, tracking, and distributing digital documents. Such systems are particularly important in organizations where documents must pass through multiple departments and approval stages.\r\nHung et al. found that electronic document management is an important component of e-government because successful implementation depends not only on technical functionality but also on user acceptance, training, compatibility, and facilitating conditions\r\nThis concept directly relates to E-Legis because legislative documents pass through several users, including the Mayor\'s Office, Secretariat, Presiding Officer, and Council Members.\r\n4. Artificial Intelligence in Public Governance\r\nArtificial intelligence has increasingly been applied to government processes to support decision-making, automation, classification, and service delivery. Sousa et al. reviewed AI applications in the public sector and found increasing interest in using AI to improve government services while also identifying ethical and policy considerations.\r\nFor E-Legis, this literature provides support for the use of AI-assisted features such as document classification, summarization, intelligent search, and speech-to-text processing.\r\n5. Artificial Intelligence and Legal Documents\r\nAriai and Demartini (2024) reviewed 127 studies involving natural language processing in the legal domain. Their review identified legal document summarization, legal text classification, named entity recognition, question answering, and legal judgment prediction as important NLP applications. The authors also emphasized challenges associated with the long and complex structure of legal documents.\r\nThis is highly relevant to E-Legis because ordinances, resolutions, minutes, and other legislative documents contain complex legal language. AI-assisted classification and summarization can help legislative personnel locate and understand important information more efficiently.\r\n6. Legal Document Summarization\r\nLegal documents are often lengthy and contain multiple sections, references, and provisions. Recent literature on legal document summarization indicates that NLP can be used to produce shorter versions of legal documents while preserving important information.\r\nGhosh et al. examined extractive and abstractive approaches to legal case-document summarization and noted that legal documents present particular challenges because of their length and specialized language.\r\nThis supports the inclusion of an AI summarization feature in E-Legis, particularly for helping users quickly review lengthy ordinances, resolutions, and session-related documents.\r\n7. Intelligent Legal Information Retrieval\r\nModern legal information systems increasingly use NLP to improve the retrieval of relevant legal provisions. Louis et al. demonstrated that legal information retrieval can be improved by considering the structural relationships between legal provisions rather than treating each document as an isolated text.\r\nThis literature supports E-Legis\'s intelligent search functionality. The system can help users locate relevant legislative records more efficiently than manually searching through physical files.\r\n8. Blockchain and Government\r\nBlockchain technology has been increasingly investigated for government applications because of its potential to improve transparency, security, and trust. A systematic review of blockchain applications in government analyzed 175 scholarly articles and found applications involving public services, electronic voting, registration, transparency, and security.\r\nThis provides theoretical support for the document-verification component of E-Legis. However, the manuscript correctly limits its blockchain-related functionality to SHA-256 hash verification, rather than claiming that the entire system is a distributed blockchain network. Your manuscript specifically states this limitation.\r\n9. Blockchain Adoption Challenges\r\nAlthough blockchain can improve security and transparency, its adoption in government organizations also presents challenges. A systematic review identified interoperability, technical expertise, and organizational factors as important barriers to blockchain adoption in public organizations.\r\nThis supports the decision of E-Legis to use a relatively lightweight hash-verification approach rather than implementing a complex public blockchain infrastructure.\r\n10. Digital Government and User-Centered Services\r\nThe OECD emphasizes that mature digital government should be user-driven and should redesign internal processes around the needs of users. Digital technologies should improve efficiency, accessibility, coordination, and transparency rather than simply replicate paper-based procedures electronically.\r\nThis principle is applicable to E-Legis because different users have different responsibilities. The Secretariat manages documents and agendas, Council Members review legislative materials, and the Presiding Officer performs approval functions.\r\nB. Local Related Literature\r\n1. Republic Act No. 7160 – Local Government Code\r\nRepublic Act No. 7160, or the Local Government Code of 1991, establishes the functions and responsibilities of local government units and their legislative bodies. The Sangguniang Panglungsod is responsible for passing ordinances and resolutions within its jurisdiction. Your manuscript uses this legal framework to establish the importance of legislative document processing in local governance.\r\nThis provides the institutional basis for E-Legis because the system is specifically designed for legislative offices such as the Sangguniang Panglungsod.\r\n2. Republic Act No. 12254 – E-Governance Act\r\nRepublic Act No. 12254 institutionalizes the Philippine government\'s transition toward e-governance. It covers national government agencies as well as local government units and promotes interoperability, digital transformation, data security, transparency, and efficient government processes.\r\nThe law is directly related to E-Legis because the proposed system digitizes internal legislative operations and supports paperless government transactions.\r\n3. Implementing Rules and Regulations of the E-Governance Act\r\nThe 2026 Implementing Rules and Regulations of Republic Act No. 12254 provide additional guidance for implementing e-governance in government organizations. The IRR emphasizes secure, integrated, and digitally enabled government services.\r\nThis strengthens the relevance of E-Legis because the system incorporates digital workflows, electronic records, access controls, and document security.\r\n4. Philippine Development Plan 2023–2028\r\nThe Philippine Development Plan 2023–2028 identifies digital transformation as an important government priority. It specifically promotes paperless, cashless, and data-empowered transactions and calls for the improvement of government processes through digital technologies.\r\nThe direction of the plan supports E-Legis because the system aims to reduce paper dependency and improve the efficiency of legislative processes.\r\n5. Data Privacy Act of 2012\r\nRepublic Act No. 10173, or the Data Privacy Act of 2012, establishes requirements for protecting personal information processed by government and private organizations. It covers the collection, storage, retrieval, modification, and other processing of personal information.\r\nThis is important to E-Legis because the system manages user accounts, attendance, legislative records, and other information that must be protected from unauthorized access.\r\n6. Data Privacy Act Implementing Rules\r\nThe National Privacy Commission\'s implementing rules emphasize security safeguards, documentation, accountability, and appropriate access to personal information.\r\nThese principles support the security requirements of E-Legis, particularly user authentication, authorization, role-based access, and secure handling of legislative records.\r\n7. Philippine Government Digital Transformation\r\nThe Philippine government has increasingly emphasized digital transformation as a means of improving public service delivery. Government policies encourage agencies and LGUs to replace fragmented manual processes with integrated digital systems.\r\nThe Philippine Development Plan identifies digitalization as a mechanism for improving efficiency, transparency, and government service delivery.\r\nThis provides a national policy foundation for the development of E-Legis.\r\n8. Government Digital Document Management\r\nIn 2026, the Department of the Interior and Local Government launched the Document Management System for LGUs (DMS4LGUs). The platform is intended to digitize and centralize government records while reducing paper-based processes and improving document tracking, security, coordination, and transparency.\r\nThe initiative demonstrates that digital document management is becoming an important component of Philippine local governance and closely supports the direction of E-Legis.\r\n9. E-Governance and Local Government Websites\r\nSiar\'s study of Philippine city government websites found that although local governments had increased their web presence, the adoption and utilization of websites as e-governance tools remained limited.\r\nThis literature suggests that simply having a website is insufficient. Government offices need systems that support actual internal workflows, document processing, and administrative functions, which is the gap E-Legis attempts to address.\r\n10. Digital Governance and Philippine LGUs\r\nRecent Philippine policy literature continues to emphasize the need for local government units to improve digital infrastructure, capacity, interoperability, and citizen-oriented services. The current national e-governance framework specifically includes LGUs and back-end government operations.\r\nTherefore, E-Legis is consistent with the country\'s broader movement toward digitally integrated local government operations.\r\n2.2 Related Studies\r\nA. Foreign Related Studies\r\n1. Hung et al. – Electronic Document Management System\r\nHung et al. investigated user acceptance of an electronic document management system in Taiwan. Using 186 users and a Theory of Planned Behavior framework, the study found that perceived usefulness, ease of use, training, compatibility, self-efficacy, and facilitating conditions affected users\' intention to use the system.\r\nThis study is relevant to E-Legis because successful implementation depends not only on developing the system but also on ensuring that legislative personnel can understand and effectively use its functions.\r\n2. Zuiderwijk, Chen, and Salem – AI in Public Governance\r\nZuiderwijk et al. conducted a systematic literature review on AI applications in public governance. Their review identified a growing body of research on AI in government but also emphasized the need for empirical research, ethical considerations, and multidisciplinary approaches.\r\nThe study supports the inclusion of AI in E-Legis while highlighting the importance of responsible implementation and evaluation.\r\n3. Sousa et al. – AI in the Public Sector\r\nSousa et al. reviewed 59 studies involving artificial intelligence in the public sector. Their findings showed increasing use of AI technologies in government services and identified both potential benefits and ethical considerations.\r\nThe findings support the use of AI-assisted document processing in E-Legis but indicate that AI functionality should be treated as decision support rather than an unrestricted replacement for human review.\r\n4. Ghosh et al. – Legal Document Summarization\r\nGhosh et al. compared extractive and abstractive approaches for legal case-document summarization. The study emphasized that legal documents are difficult to summarize because they are lengthy and contain complex information.\r\nThe findings are relevant to the AI summarization component of E-Legis, particularly for reducing the time required to review lengthy legislative documents.\r\n5. Louis et al. – Statutory Article Retrieval\r\nLouis, van Dijck, and Spanakis developed a graph-augmented approach for retrieving relevant statutory articles. Their experiments demonstrated improvements over strong retrieval baselines by incorporating the structural organization of legislation.\r\nThis study supports the intelligent-search functionality proposed for E-Legis because legislative users need to locate relevant ordinances, resolutions, and provisions efficiently.\r\n6. Ma et al. – Legal Case Retrieval\r\nMa et al. developed CaseEncoder, a knowledge-enhanced model for legal case retrieval. The researchers found that general-purpose language models may not adequately capture legal characteristics and therefore proposed legal-specific training methods.\r\nThis is relevant to E-Legis because legislative documents contain specialized terminology and structures. AI features should therefore be designed with consideration for the characteristics of legislative texts.\r\n7. Nguyen et al. – Legal Text Retrieval\r\nNguyen et al. investigated joint learning for legal text retrieval and textual entailment. Their empirical findings showed that combining information about document relevance and legal conclusions can improve legal information retrieval.\r\nThe study supports the development of intelligent legislative search because relevant information may depend on relationships among different sections and documents.\r\n8. Abidin and Munggaran – Blockchain Document Verification\r\nAbidin and Munggaran studied blockchain-based digital document verification using SHA-256. Their work demonstrated the usefulness of cryptographic hashing for verifying document integrity. Your manuscript already identifies this source as part of the technical foundation of the project.\r\nThis directly supports the E-Legis feature that calculates and compares document hashes to detect unauthorized modifications.\r\n9. Blockchain Adoption in Government Organizations\r\nA systematic review of blockchain adoption in government organizations identified 72 factors influencing adoption. The study emphasized that technological, organizational, and environmental factors all affect whether blockchain solutions can successfully be implemented.\r\nThe findings support E-Legis\'s limited implementation of blockchain-inspired verification rather than attempting to deploy a complete distributed blockchain infrastructure.\r\n10. Electronic Records Management in Government\r\nA study of electronic records management implementation in a government land board examined the organizational and human factors affecting adoption. The research used questionnaires, interviews, and document reviews to assess implementation.\r\nThe findings demonstrate that technology alone does not guarantee successful electronic records management. Organizational readiness, user knowledge, and change management are also important considerations for E-Legis.\r\nB. Local Related Studies\r\n1. Cruz and Balahadia – Laguna Legislative Management\r\nCruz and Balahadia (2023) developed a legislative management and tracking system for the Province of Laguna using the Latent Dirichlet Allocation algorithm. The study explored the use of machine learning to identify themes from previous ordinances and resolutions. Your manuscript identifies this study as evidence that AI techniques can be applied to Philippine legislative records.\r\nThe study is highly relevant to E-Legis because both systems use intelligent processing to improve access to legislative documents.\r\n2. Gabuya – Paperless Legislative Sessions\r\nGabuya (2025) examined the readiness of the Sangguniang Bayan of LGU Motiong to transition from paper-based sessions to paperless legislative operations. The study surveyed 13 legislative personnel and considered digital tools, digital literacy, familiarity with paperless platforms, and institutional support.\r\nThis study is directly related to E-Legis because it demonstrates that Philippine legislative offices are actively considering the transition toward digital and paperless sessions.\r\n3. Rodriguez et al. – eDALAYON\r\nRodriguez et al. (2024) developed eDALAYON, a document management and monitoring system for the Department of the Interior and Local Government in the Philippines. The study addressed document management and monitoring within a government setting. Your manuscript cites this research as evidence that manual document management can result in delays, lost routing information, and reduced transparency.\r\nThe study supports E-Legis because both systems focus on improving document monitoring and government workflow.\r\n4. Gamido et al. – Electronic Document Management\r\nGamido, Gamido, and Macaspac (2023) investigated an electronic document management system for LAN-based organizations. The study demonstrates the usefulness of electronic document management for organizing and retrieving organizational records.\r\nThis relates to E-Legis because legislative documents can be centralized in a database and retrieved without depending on physical filing systems.\r\n5. Lagman et al. – Digital Governance Information System\r\nLagman et al. (2025) developed and evaluated an enterprise-level information system for digital governance in Philippine state universities and colleges using Agile methodology and ISO/IEC 25010. The study is relevant to E-Legis because it demonstrates the applicability of Agile development and software-quality evaluation within a Philippine institutional setting.\r\nThe study supports the methodology used by E-Legis, particularly iterative development and software quality assessment.\r\n6. LeMTrac – Legislative Management and Tracking System\r\nThe LeMTrac study developed a legislative management and tracking system for municipal legislative offices in Nabua and Bula, Camarines Sur. The system was evaluated by end-users and IT experts and received a high acceptability rating, particularly for usability. It also improved document accessibility, security, and management.\r\nLeMTrac is one of the most closely related studies to E-Legis because both focus specifically on legislative document management and workflow tracking in Philippine local government.\r\n7. Lagura – Digital Governance Divide in Philippine Cities\r\nLagura (2025) assessed the websites of 33 highly urbanized Philippine cities and identified gaps involving content quality, privacy, inclusivity, public engagement, and technical infrastructure.\r\nThe study indicates that Philippine LGUs have varying levels of digital governance maturity. E-Legis addresses a more specialized need by providing an internal legislative workflow system rather than relying solely on public-facing websites.\r\n8. Dela Peña – E-Governance in Paracale\r\nDela Peña examined e-government services in the Local Government Unit of Paracale, Camarines Norte. The study evaluated functionality, reliability, usability, performance efficiency, compatibility, maintainability, portability, and security using the ISO 25010 standard.\r\nThis study is relevant to E-Legis because the current manuscript also evaluates system quality using ISO/IEC 25010.\r\n9. Valiente – Legislative Records Management System\r\nValiente developed a proposed Legislative Records Management System for the Sangguniang Bayan of Garchitorena. The study identified problems associated with manual \r\nand paper-based records, including delayed retrieval, limited accessibility, risk of record loss, and operational inefficiency.\r\nThis study strongly supports E-Legis because the identified problems are similar to those stated in the E-Legis project context.\r\n10. Legislative Management and E-Session System for Motiong\r\nA 2026 study developed a Legislative Management and E-Session System for the Sangguniang Bayan of LGU Motiong, Samar. The proposed web-based system integrates legislative management and electronic session functions to address delays, document-management difficulties, and problems associated with paper-based processes.\r\nThis study is particularly relevant to E-Legis because it demonstrates the growing development of web-based legislative and e-session systems in Philippine local government.\r\nReference:\r\n Abidin, M. F., & Munggaran, L. C. (2025). \r\nBlockchain-based digital document verification using SHA-256 on the Internet Computer Protocol (ICP).\r\n \r\n Ariai, F., & Demartini, G. (2024). \r\nNatural language processing for the legal domain: A survey of tasks, datasets, models, and challenges.\r\n \r\n  Cruz, C. A., & Balahadia, F. F. (2023). \r\nProvince of Laguna legislative management and tracking system with the application of Latent Dirichlet Allocation (LDA) algorithm.\r\n \r\nGabuya, R. J. B. (2025). \r\nTowards digital legislation: Readiness of the Sangguniang Bayan of LGU Motiong for paperless sessions.\r\n \r\n Gamido, M. V., Gamido, H. V., & Macaspac, D. J. P. (2023). \r\nElectronic document management system for local area network-based organizations.\r\n \r\n Ghosh, K., Goyal, P., & Ghosh, S. (2022). \r\nLegal case document summarization: Extractive and abstractive methods and their evaluation.\r\n \r\n Lagman, A. C., et al. (2025). \r\nDevelopment and evaluation of an enterprise-level information system for digital governance in Philippine SUCs using agile software methodology and ISO/IEC 25010 software quality model.\r\n \r\nOECD. (2020). \r\nThe OECD Digital Government Policy Framework: Six dimensions of a digital government.\r\n \r\n Republic Act No. 10173. (2012). \r\nData Privacy Act of 2012.\r\n \r\nRepublic Act No. 12254. (2025). \r\nE-Governance Act.\r\n \r\nRodriguez, B., III, et al. (2024). \r\neDALAYON: A document management and monitoring system for the Department of the Interior and Local Government, Philippines.\r\n \r\nSiar, S. V. (2007). \r\nE-governance at the local government level in the Philippines: An assessment of city government websites.\r\n \r\nSousa, W. G., et al. (2019). \r\nHow and where is artificial intelligence in the public sector going? A literature review and research agenda.\r\n \r\nValiente, S. M. N. (2026). \r\nDesign of a Legislative Records Management System: A phased intervention for the Sangguniang Bayan of Garchitorena.\r\n \r\nZuiderwijk, A., Chen, Y.-C., & Salem, F. (2021). \r\nImplications of the use of artificial intelligence in public governance: A systematic literature review and a research agenda.', 'rrlrrs.docx', '1781889b6c929828_rrlrrs.docx', '9b08cbbe5fa46822f20497ab7de8ca7c77f6dc7725b2f3810ddda543df9cd89d', '7486bc1d2df7e69c', 'isu', '2026-09-22 22:03:26.000000', 'Baseline snapshot migrated from existing record', NULL, '99d2ff63c69bc204', 'Revision v1'),
('e52d99c909ce2dc8', '', 2, 'Republic of the Philippines', 'Republic of the Philippines<br><br>City of Santiago<br><br>-000-<br><br>OFFICE OF THE SECRETARY - SANGGUNIANG PANLUNGSOD<br><br>EXCERPT FROM THE MINUTES OF THE REGULAR SESSION OF THE 12TH CITY COUNCIL HELD ON SEPTEMBER 29, 2025 AT AMPHITHEATER, BARANGAY HALL,<br><br>SAN ANDRES, SANTIAGO CITY<br><br>APPROVED UNDER SUSPENDED RULES<br><br>Sponsored by: Coun.Joan Mae V. De Jesus<br><br>Co-sponsored by: Coun. Cassandra Eunice Sable<br><br>Coun. Tomirose Bautista-Dela Cruz<br><br>Coun. Sherman A. Miguel<br><br>Coun. Anton Phoenix A. Abaya<br><br>Coun.Jay Arcie S. Co<br><br>Coun. Russel D. Ponce<br><br>Coun. Gino Antonio A. Siquian<br><br>Coun. Carlo N. De Luna<br><br>Coun. Orlando T. Chan<br><br>Coun. Benzi Tan Chai<br><br>Coun. Christopher G. Respicio Coun. Bella M. Yodyod<br><br>RESOLUTION NO. 12THCC-051<br><br>A RESOLUTION ENCOURAGING ALL TOURISM-RELATED ESTABLISHMENTS WITHIN THE CITY OF SANTIAGO TO ESTABLISH THEIR OWN ONE TOWN, ONE PRODUCT (OTOP) KIOSKS<br><br>WHEREAS, Republic Act No. 11960, otherwise known as the \"OTOP Philippines Act, institutionalizes the One Town, One Product Program as a national strategy to promote inclusive local economic development, support micro, small, and medium enterprises (MSMEs), and preserve cultural heritage through the advancement of community-based products and services;<br><br>WHEREAS, the Department of Trade and Industry (DTI), in partnership with local government units (LGUs), has established OTOP Hubs to serve as centralized venues for the marketing, distribution, and promotion of locally crafted goods, processed food items, and skill-based services, among others;<br><br>WHEREAS, tourism-related establishments—including but not limited to hotels, resorts, restaurants, travel agencies, and pasalubong centers—are strategically positioned to serve as platforms for showcasing the City of Santiago\'s unique local products to both domestic and international visitors;<br><br>WHEREAS, the integration of OTOP kiosks within such establishments is expected to enhance product visibility, foster cultural pride, and stimulate economic activity by providing local artisans and entrepreneurs with direct access to consumer markets;<br><br>WHEREAS, to uphold the integrity and objectives of the OTOP Philippines Program, all products to be displayed and sold in said kiosks shall be sourced exclusively from the officially designated OTOP Hub of the City, and must be supplied by duly accredited MSMEs operating within the locality;<br><br>WHEREFORE, on motion of Coun. Joan Mae V. De Jesus, unanimously seconded, be it —<br><br>RESOLVED, as it is hereby resolved, to encourage all tourism-related establishments operating within the territorial jurisdiction of the City of Santiago to establish their own One Town<br><br>One Product (OTOP) kiosks, in support of ocal e rprise development a d cultural promotion;<br><br>on No. 12t<br><br>RESOLVED FURTHER, that products to be displayed and sold in said kiosks shall be sourced from the officially designated OTOP Hub of the City, thereby ensuring quality, authenticity, and alignment with the standards and objectives of the OTOP Philippines Program;<br><br>RESOLVED FINALLY, that copies of this Resolution be furnished to the Department of Trade and Industry, the Santiago City Information, Culture and the Arts, and Tourism Office (CICATO), and all concerned tourism-related establishments for their information, guidance, and appropriate action.<br><br>ADOPTED. SEPTEMBER 29, 2025.<br><br>JESUS<br><br>RUSSE<br><br>CARL <br><br>LUNA<br><br>JESUS<br><br>RUSSE<br><br>CARL <br><br>LUNA<br><br>TOMIROSE <br><br>CRUZ<br><br>ABAYA<br><br>SIQUIAN<br><br>RESPICIO<br><br>ity <br><br>TOMIROSE <br><br>CRUZ<br><br>ABAYA<br><br>SIQUIAN<br><br>RESPICIO<br><br>ity <br><br> CASSA CE SABLE<br><br> SHE A A GUEL<br><br>JOA<br><br>ONCE<br><br>City Councilor<br><br><u>EX-OFFICIO MEMBERS:</u><br><br>1 NCHAI<br><br> (Pr Ide <u>i</u><u> a </u>Mg Barangay)<br><br>City Councilor<br><br>BELL M. ODYOD<br><br>(IPMandat ry Representative)<br><br>City Councilor<br><br>CORRECT:<br><br>CERTIFIED <br><br>CERTIFIED <br><br>ATTE<u>STE</u>D:<br><br>NE<br><br>TILLO TAN<br><br>City Vic r & Presiding Officer<br><br>APPROVED:<br><br>NA or<br><br>ATTY. ALYSSA SH P. TAN, CPA<br><br>City M <br><br>Page 2 of2 pages ofResolution No. 12thCC-051', 'R12thCC-051 Encouraging all Tourism Related Establishments within the City of Santiago to Establish their own One Town One Product OTOP Kiosks.docx', 'revision_532ac15691dedc0c_v2_5fcc7e39a1b0dd28_R12thCC-051_Encouraging_all_Tourism_Related_Establishments_within_the_City_of_Santiago_to_Establish_their_own_One_Town_One_Product_OTOP_Kiosks.docx', 'bffc5281a9f96e3ee13bf715f92646c6b1b930c40324a1807afdabc478287eaa', '7486bc1d2df7e69c', 'isu', '2026-10-06 02:07:28.000000', NULL, NULL, '532ac15691dedc0c', 'Signed v2');
